In this 1989 NLSY79 young-adult sample (ages 24-32), older age is associated with a lower likelihood of dissaving (withdrawing more from savings than putting in). Each additional year of age is associated with about 4% lower odds of dissaving (OR ~0.96/yr, 95% CI 0.94-0.98, p<0.001; AME ~ -0.008 probability units/yr). The profile is weakly non-linear: predicted dissaving probability peaks near age 25-26 then declines roughly monotonically from ~0.26 at 24-26 to ~0.19 at 32. The gradient persists after controlling for income, gender, race and BMI, and holds under probit, age-banding and BMI-winsorization checks. Caveat: the negative gradient is concentrated in the ~31% of rows with (imputed) mean income; within the non-imputed 69% it is flat-to-positive and non-significant, so the association is fragile to INCOME data quality and strictly correlational.
